WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.634
Chunking turns source material into retrievable pieces,

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:05.531
but it is not a neutral formatting step.

00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:09.151
The wrong chunking strategy can remove context, mix unrelated topics,

00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:12.772
split requirements from exceptions, separate a table from its explanation,

00:00:12.772 --> 00:00:17.840
or detach a statement from the source rules that control who may see it.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.012
Good chunking follows structure and meaning.

00:00:20.012 --> 00:00:22.909
Headings, sections, lists, tables, version notes, policy clauses,

00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:26.529
and natural topic boundaries should influence how content is divided.

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:29.788
A chunk should be small enough to retrieve precisely,

00:00:29.788 --> 00:00:32.322
but large enough to keep useful context.

00:00:32.322 --> 00:00:34.856
When a document contains exceptions or conditions,

00:00:34.856 --> 00:00:38.839
those details should not be separated from the rule they qualify.

00:00:38.839 --> 00:00:40.287
Metadata makes chunks governable.

00:00:40.287 --> 00:00:43.907
Useful fields include source document ID, title, owner, classification, tenant,

00:00:43.907 --> 00:00:48.252
section heading, reference URL, ingestion date, modified date, effective date, expiration date,

00:00:48.252 --> 00:00:49.700
lifecycle status, and permissions.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.958
These fields support filtering, ranking, review, and eventual cleanup.

00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:58.027
Provenance answers the practical questions: where did this come from, who may see it,

00:00:58.027 --> 00:00:59.837
and is it still current?

00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:03.096
Citations and source references are not just user-interface polish.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:06.354
They support accountability, correction, expert review, and user confidence.

00:01:06.354 --> 00:01:10.336
Without provenance, a good answer can still be hard to trust.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:14.681
Private RAG architecture should keep every chunk attached to its source context,

00:01:14.681 --> 00:01:16.491
lifecycle status, and access rules.

00:01:16.491 --> 00:01:21.560
The retrieval layer should be able to explain not only what text was selected,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.904
but also why that text was eligible for the user and workflow.

00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:27.353
Chunking also needs maintenance.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:30.249
As documents are revised, renamed, merged, or retired,

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:34.593
derived chunks should be refreshed so the index does not preserve old

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:38.938
fragments that no longer match the source record or the current permission

00:01:38.938 --> 00:01:39.300
model.
